# What a change reaches, and what answers for it.
#
# The suite is selected two ways. The cost profiles in meson.build
# select on what a test costs and say nothing about what it is for, so
# `full` runs every test the tree carries whatever was edited. That is
# the right selection for a verdict and the wrong one for an edit: a
# paragraph reworded in doc/ is re-verified against the banding
# campaign, the corpus harnesses and three hundred other tests that
# cannot see it.
#
# This table is the other axis. It says which part of the tree each area
# of the suite answers for, so a change is gated against the tests that
# could notice it. tests/gate.sh reads it in both directions -- a path
# to the area it belongs to, an area to the tests that guard it -- and
# there is one table rather than two lists, so the two directions cannot
# come to disagree.
#
# ---- the form
#
# Four kinds of line, each naming its area first:
#
#   <area> path  <glob>   a part of the tree this area answers for
#   <area> test  <glob>   a test that answers for this area
#   <area> testif <glob>  the same, for a test a configuration may not
#                         define at all -- a window device needs a
#                         display server, so a build without one defines
#                         no test that drives it. Selects exactly as test
#                         does; the difference is that a test rule is
#                         held to naming a test that exists, so that a
#                         renamed test cannot leave its rule behind, and
#                         a testif rule cannot be held to that.
#   <area> width <policy> what the wide-object build runs for it
#   <area> scope all      a change here is gated on the whole suite
#
# In a glob `*` matches within one path element and `**` matches across
# them, so `data/*.ps` is the PostScript beside init and `src/**` is
# everything under src. Blank lines and lines opening with # are
# nothing.
#
# ---- how a path finds its area
#
# The path lines are ORDERED and the FIRST that matches wins, so every
# file belongs to exactly one area and the areas are written most
# specific first. The last rules in the table are catch-alls naming
# `everything`, which is what makes the table safe to leave incomplete:
# a source file added tomorrow and not classified here falls through to
# a catch-all and gates against the whole suite at both widths, which is
# what this tree did before the table existed. So the table can cost a
# developer a saving it might have had. It cannot cost anyone a test.
#
# That is the direction to keep it in. Where a file plausibly belongs to
# two areas it is classified as the broader of them, and where its reach
# is not obvious it is left to the catch-all.
#
# ---- how an area finds its tests
#
# The test lines are a UNION and are not ordered: a test may guard
# several areas and runs when a change reaches any of them. A glob
# matches a whole test name as the build lists it.
#
# Every test the build defines must be named by some area other than
# `width`, and tests/check-gate-map.sh holds the table to that. `width`
# is excluded because nothing a change touches lands there: it is what
# the wide build runs, not a region of the tree, so a test named only in
# it would never be run by the narrow build -- the primary one -- under
# any per-change gate. It is what stops a
# test becoming unreachable: a test in no area is one no proportionate
# selection ever runs, and the whole point of selecting is undone by one
# that nothing selects. The guard holds the other side too -- a path
# rule matching no file and a test rule matching no test are both stale,
# and a stale rule is one that quietly stopped selecting what it names.
#
# `scope all` is how an area says the whole suite answers for it, and it
# is kept apart from the test lines for that reason. An area could say
# the same thing by naming every test with a bare `*`, and then every
# test in the tree would be named by it and the rule above would be
# satisfied by an area that classifies nothing. Three areas carry
# `scope all`: the object and its memory, the build description, and the
# catch-all. Two of them go on naming their own tests as well, because
# what they select and what answers for them are different questions.
#
# ---- what the wide build runs
#
# The tree builds at two object widths. The narrow one is primary and
# both are equally important; neither is dropped here. What this settles
# is HOW MUCH of the wide build one change is gated against, which is a
# different question from whether the wide build is gated at all.
#
#   width full      the change reaches the layer that decides the width,
#                   so the wide build runs the whole of what was
#                   selected: the object, the memory it lives in, and
#                   the build's own configuration.
#
#   width witness   everything else. The wide build runs the tests that
#                   read the width directly -- the `width` area below --
#                   whatever the change was. They are cheap, they run on
#                   every gate, and they are a tripwire rather than a
#                   verdict: they do not stand in for a wide run of the
#                   change's own tests, which is what the batch gate is
#                   for.
#
# The batch gate, `tests/gate.sh --batch`, is both widths over
# everything, and is what a branch is held to before it goes anywhere.
# The per-change gate is for the edit-run-edit loop in between.

